 show the race tied or Salazar up only a point amid immigration-focused attacks", "Salazar holds a large financial edge, with $2.26 million on hand to Rodriguez's $325,000", "A Democratic-favorable national environment offsets the district's six-point Republican lean", "Trump's public rift with Salazar and criticism of her immigration record complicate her standing with Cuban-American voters"], "rationale": "The most recent live-caller polls show a dead heat, and headlines about immigration politics and Trump's attacks on Salazar point to real tightening, so the Republican edge is narrower than raters' Lean R to Likely R consensus implies. Salazar's incumbency, cash advantage, and the district's Republican lean still make her the favorite.", "watch": "Any independent polling or further escalation of the Trump-Salazar rift could move Cuban-American voters and shift the margin either way.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent María Elvira Salazar (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Freshman Republican Maria Elvira Salazar seeks another term in this Miami-Dade district that leans Republican by about six points. Democratic nominee Eliott Rodriguez, a former news anchor, has centered the race on immigration politics, and recent polls from Democratic-leaning firms show the contest essentially tied, a shift from earlier surveys that gave Salazar a modest edge. Ten race raters still call the district Lean or Likely Republican, and prediction markets give Democrats roughly a one-in-three chance.\n\nSalazar holds a decisive financial advantage, with $2.3 million on hand to Rodriguez's $325,000, and incumbency typically adds a cushion. But a public rift with President Trump, criticism of her immigration record among Cuban-American voters, and a favorable national environment for Democrats have kept the race competitive. The seat remains the incumbent's to lose, though her margin of safety has narrowed in the campaign's final weeks.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p